A||||15||extinct||using the defog switch as an aux. manual radiato fan switch?||||1||
Z||000000||extinct||11-28-2001||02:18 AM||||My car does not have the rear defog. If one purchased a trim plate and defog switch, could you wire it as an auxiliary radiator fan switch? I have seen articles that have you splice into the green/white wire under the dash and wire it into a toggle switch that completes the ground for the radiator fan. I would rather use a stock looking switch than an aftermarket switch.<P>Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||camon||11-28-2001||02:40 AM||||can't see why it wouldn't.<P>Run the hot wire to the green/white stripe wire and the ground to chasis.<P><BR>The switch shouldn't matter as long as it has an on and off postion!<BR>------------------<BR>Keith<p>[This message has been edited by camon (edited 11-28-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||extinct||11-28-2001||02:59 AM||||If Iam not mistaken, the defog switch also has a light on it. You could also wire it up to a power source so that the light come on when the switch is pressed to turn on the radiator fan!||||reg||1
Z||000003||rogergarrison||11-28-2001||06:47 AM||||make sure you got the right 'hot' wire, the switch is on a timer that turns off after a few minutes if its plugged into its harness.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Phil||11-28-2001||06:56 AM||||I could be wrong but I think that the defog switch is a momentary contact switch. As soon as you take your finger off the switch the circuit opens.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Eriamjh||11-28-2001||07:11 AM||||Make sure that the current that drives the fan is going through a relay and not the switch itself.  That switch is probably not designed to handle the current that the fan will pull.<P>I'm sure the timer that the rear defog would have is also a relay output, meaning only a small amount of current goes through the switch itself.||||reg||1
Z||000006||extinct||11-28-2001||10:03 AM||||Its my understanding that there is no "hot wire".  All the switch does is complete the ground that normally would happen when the coolant temp switch is activated. Here is a quote from Paul Vargus explaination on the online service guide:<P>Battery voltage is supplied at all times to the coolant fan relay. The fan (or fans incl the rear fans in those so equipped) is energized by merely providing a ground. This is done when you put the A/C in MAX. NORM, or B/L, or the A/C High pressure cut out switch. When the coolant temperature exceeds 235F. The coolant temperature switch closes, and provides the ground to the relay coil to be energized. If you want to switch your coolant fan on whenever you want, all you have to do is manually provide the ground through a switch. Find the main wiring junction block on the front firewall above and forward of the brake pedal (inside the car!) Find the dark green and white stripe wire in plug F9 (4th up from the bottom on the left side) and splice into it through a switch, to ground. (Do not cut the green/white wire). When the switch is turned on, with the ignition on, this will ground the relay, which activates the fan in the same way the coolant temp switch & A/C switch does.<P>so has anyone used the defog switch?||||reg||1
Z||000007||Phil||11-28-2001||11:20 AM||||You can't just use a defog switch alone. You would have to build a timing circuit, unless you want to keep your finger on the switch all the time.||||reg||1
Z||000008||terryk||11-28-2001||11:39 AM||||Or set up a self latching relay.||||reg||1
Z||000009||jimmybpei||11-28-2001||01:07 PM||||if i am not mistake the fan will come on but only for a little while. It seem tha t the defog switch is on a timer, i too have thought of this switch for driving lites. <BR>Works great for about five min then they shut off, push the switch a gain and thhey shut off again. Havent gone any furthur with it, there mus be a way ariund it.<P>Thats just my experience with the defog switch.<P>Later <BR>Jimmy B.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Phil||11-28-2001||02:38 PM||||The timing unit is a separate unit(up under the dash in rear window defroster equipped cars) and is not in the switch itself.||||reg||1
Z||000011||jimmybpei||11-28-2001||06:14 PM||||Ok thanks Phil.<P>I'll remember that when i put in the new driving lite, one got smashed out. I didnt like the idea of cutting the dash for a switch. I love the look of the interior stock. <P>Thanks <BR>Jimmy B.||||reg||1
Z||000012||skitime||11-28-2001||06:55 PM||||Ok, here is what I did.  I use the defrost switch for the switch controlling my driving lights in my chin spoiler.  I got the defrost wiring harness from a wrecked car including the relay.  I then installed the defrost harness connecting the low amperage relay power for the defrost relay circuit to the light circuit so then it will only be on if the parking lights are on and the blue dingy thing will warn me if it is left on. I then replaced the timer relay with a normal relay so the driving lights will stay on as long as the relay is energized by the defrost switch.  Now here is the last trick.  Take the defrost switch rocker off and remove the spring. It is easily removed and reassembled.  Now you have a factory looking switch that will stay on and light up brightly when the driving lights are on and dimly when the parking light are on.  One last application that I have not had time to do is make use of the second set of contacts in the switch. I will make the switch momentary in one direction and a toggle on in the driving light direction. I want to solder wires to it so that the momentary switch will activate my garage door opener so that I can have the opener permanently out of site. <P>------------------<BR><UL TYPE=SQUARE><BR><LI>Red 88 GT T-TOP<BR><LI>Red 86 GT<BR></UL>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature-2.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000013||theogre||11-29-2001||12:03 AM||||The switch for the rear defog is a "single pole double throw with forced center off." Pushing the switch will only close one contact or the other monentarily.<P>You have to build a latching circuit like Terry said or physically modify the switch. The latching circuit would be better. Modification of the switch won't be fun and may not even be posible w/o somehow damaging the switch.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000014||skitime||11-29-2001||07:00 AM||||Theogre, the switch is easily modified and I explain how in my last post. It can be modified into a single pole double throw switch and the forced center off can be removed.  ||||reg||1
Z||000015||ka4nkf||11-29-2001||09:04 PM||||The best thing you can do is buy Rodney's 210 on and 200 off fan switch. I promise you this will cure your hot problems. My Fiero never reaches 220 even if you let it idle for a long time. also you still have your 195 temp thermostat. The Fiero runs better at 195 degrees than it does at 160 degrees.<BR>anyway I promise this will cure your problem.||||reg||1
